#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Catalogue every document the corpus holds, and emit it for the browser.

Seven catalog pages and twelve collection pages between them link every PDF
this project builds, and none of them answers the questions a reader actually
arrives with: how long is it, when was it last revised, which model wrote it,
can I read it without downloading anything. Those facts are in the tree — in a
preamble, a provenance ledger, an eligibility record and a release record, one
of each per document — and until this tool existed nothing gathered them.

    check      every derived title is the one the built PDF carries
    list       the corpus, filtered, as text or YAML or JSON
    show       one document's complete record
    structure  write the catalogue the reading page fetches

The derivation is scripts/_corpus.py and lives there rather than here, because
the release builder and the provenance gate read parts of it too — `make
check-public-alpha` and `make check-metadata` — and a second walk of the same
records is a second answer waiting to differ from the first. This file decides
only what a catalogue row looks like.

Nothing here is scraped in the browser. The page fetches one generated file and
renders it; a page that walked the corpus itself would be a fifth reader of the
same records, running somewhere no check can reach it.
"""
